## 3. Detailed Comparison

| Dimension | Prompt Engineering | Loop Engineering |
|---|---|---|
| **A. What is it** | Crafting a single input to get a better single output from a model. A request/response exchange. | Designing the automated control flow — observe, plan, execute, evaluate, decide — that lets an agent keep working toward a goal across many steps without a human driving each one. |
| **B. Why use it** | It's how you communicate intent to a model and how you explore what a model can actually do before you automate anything around it. Cheap, fast, no infrastructure. | Needed once volume, multi-step dependencies, or unattended operation make "a human reviews every output" impractical. Turns one-off help into something that runs, checks itself, and scales. |
| **C. How to use it (tools / techniques)** | Zero-shot / few-shot prompting, chain-of-thought instructions, role prompting, system prompts, output-format constraints. Manual iteration: write → read → rewrite. | Automations & schedulers (cron, `/loop`, `/goal`, hooks, GitHub Actions), worktrees for isolation, `SKILL.md` files for codified project knowledge, MCP plugins/connectors for real-tool access, sub-agents for a maker/checker split, and a persistent state store (markdown file, Linear/issue board, repo) as memory across runs. In code, this is often an explicit loop class with `observe/plan/execute/evaluate` and a max-iteration guard, or a framework like LangGraph/CrewAI. |
| **D. When to use it** | Single-turn or short-turn products (chatbots, one-off generation); simple tasks solvable in one pass (summarize, classify, translate); low, human-reviewable volume; the exploration phase before you know what "good output" even looks like. | Multi-step, interdependent tasks (research → draft → fact-check → publish); tasks needing an automated quality gate at scale; anything with no human available at each step (3am jobs, event-triggered pipelines, high-volume processing); coordinating multiple agents where someone/something has to route and arbitrate between them. |
| **Human's role** | Present and active at every iteration — writes, reads, judges, rewrites. | Sets the objective, designs the evaluation criteria, and reviews at checkpoints — not every step. Review bandwidth becomes the real ceiling on how many parallel loops you can run. |
| **Where planning happens** | In the human's head, turn by turn. | Encoded into the system (or delegated to a planning step the agent runs itself), revised automatically on failure. |
| **Error recovery** | Human notices the problem and writes a new prompt. | System detects a failed evaluation and replans/retries itself, up to a defined limit. |
| **Memory model** | Conversation context only — gone once the session ends. | Persistent state outside the conversation (file, board, repo) that survives between runs; this is the piece every long-running agent depends on. |
| **Typical failure mode** | A mediocre single output; fixed by rewriting the prompt. | Runaway execution (no termination condition), silent drift from weak verification, state bloat, unhandled tool failures — systems-engineering problems, not prompting problems. |
| **Skill you're building** | Clear writing, model intuition, output-format control. | Architecture, control-flow/branching logic, evaluation design, memory management, multi-agent coordination. |
| **Relationship to each other** | Not mutually exclusive. Prompt engineering happens *inside* every node of a loop — every plan, tool call, and self-check is still a well- or poorly-written prompt. Loop engineering is the architecture; prompt engineering is the quality of each individual brick. | |

