## 5. Quick Decision Guide

Reach for **prompt engineering** when:
- It's a single-turn interaction and a person is right there to judge the output.
- The task is simple enough to nail in one pass (summarize, classify, translate).
- Volume is low enough that manual review isn't a bottleneck.
- You're still exploring what the model can do — you don't yet know what "good" looks like.

Reach for **loop engineering** when:
- The task is a chain of interdependent steps (research → outline → draft → fact-check).
- Output needs to clear a consistent quality bar at a volume no human can review one-by-one.
- The work has to run with nobody watching (scheduled jobs, event-triggered pipelines).
- You're coordinating more than one agent and something has to own the routing, retries, and stop condition.

## 6. Glossary

- **Loop** — a recursive goal: define a purpose, let the agent iterate until a checkable condition is true.
- **Harness engineering** — designing the environment a single agent runs inside; loop engineering sits one layer above this.
- **`/goal`** — an in-session primitive (present in both Claude Code and Codex) that keeps an agent working across turns until a separate, fresh model check confirms a stopping condition is genuinely met — the same maker/checker split applied to the "are we done" question itself.
- **Maker/checker split** — one agent produces the work, a different agent (or a different model, or a fresh context) verifies it, because a model is a weak judge of its own output.
- **Comprehension debt** — the growing gap between what your codebase actually does and what you personally understand, widened by shipping loop-generated code without reading it.
- **Intent debt** — project conventions and unstated context an agent has to re-guess every session if they aren't written down somewhere it can read (a skill file is intent paid off once, reused every run).
